---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How long does DNS propagation take?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Most changes propagate in 1\u201324 hours, governed by the old record\u2019s TTL. Nameserver changes can take up to 48 hours because registries cache delegation.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can I speed up DNS propagation?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes, before the change: lower your TTL to 300 seconds at least 24 hours ahead. After the change there is nothing to speed up, caches expire on their own schedule.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Why do I still see the old site?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'A cache between you and the authoritative server still holds the old record. Flush your local OS and browser caches first; if it persists elsewhere, wait out the TTL.' } },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout title="DNS Propagation: How Long It Takes and Why" description="DNS propagation explained: why changes take minutes to 48 hours, the role of TTL, timelines by record type, and how to check propagation live." schema={[faq]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DNS Propagation</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNS Propagation</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      "Propagation" is the gap between <em>you</em> seeing a DNS change and <em>the whole internet</em>
      seeing it. It exists because DNS is aggressively cached, and the cache lifetimes are set by you.
    </p>

    <Diagram name="propagation" caption="Propagation is the TTL window: caches worldwide serve old answers until each expires" />

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>Why it isn't instant</h2>
      <p>
        When you change a record, the authoritative server answers correctly immediately. But thousands
        of resolvers worldwide still hold the old answer in cache, and each keeps it until its
        <strong>TTL expires</strong>. Propagation isn't a delay in DNS, it's the waiting-out of every
        cache that stored the previous value.
      </p>

      <h2>Typical timelines</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>New record (no old value)</strong>, minutes; caches had nothing to hold.</li>
        <li><strong>Changed record, TTL 300</strong>, usually under 10 minutes everywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Changed record, TTL 3600–86400</strong>, 1 to 24 hours.</li>
        <li><strong>Nameserver (NS) changes</strong>, up to 48 hours: registrars and TLD registries cache delegation independently of your TTL.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Do it right: the migration checklist</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>24+ hours before:</strong> lower TTL to 300 on the records you'll change.</li>
        <li><strong>During:</strong> keep the old server running; make the DNS change.</li>
        <li><strong>After:</strong> watch the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a> until resolvers agree, then <a href="/flush-dns-cache/">flush your local caches</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Later:</strong> raise TTLs back up (fewer queries, faster answers).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Check propagation right now</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">checker</a> queries your record across independent
        public resolvers. When they all return the same answer, and it's your new one, propagation
        is complete.
      </p>

      
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is dns propagation in simple terms?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Think of it as the rulebook that a specific part of the internet follows. Every page in this section explains one rule, why it exists, and how to follow it correctly.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need to memorize all of this?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: bookmark this page and the related tools. The goal is understanding what to check and when, not memorizing syntax.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long does it take to implement?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Most configurations take 5-15 minutes in your DNS panel. The verification (using our tools) takes another 2 minutes.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if I get it wrong?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Depends on the setting: some errors cause immediate visible failures, others silently degrade deliverability or security. Every guide includes the symptoms to watch for.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is this the same across all DNS providers?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The underlying protocol is identical; the dashboard layout and terminology differ. Our provider playbooks cover the exact flow for Cloudflare, GoDaddy, Namecheap, Route 53, and others.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Where can I get help if I'm stuck?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Each guide links to the relevant free tool so you can verify your work. The support page lists email channels for questions the guides don't cover.</p></details></div>
<h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/what-is-ttl-in-dns/">TTL explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/site-down-after-dns-change/">Site down after a DNS change?</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
